WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.654
Time flew quickly and before long, Jo’s second story, entitled "Beautiful Strangers," was published in the Eagle (the name of the newspaper).

2
00:00:08.655 --> 00:00:14.137
The editor of the paper liked Jo’s stories so much that he bought four more of her works.

3
00:00:14.138 --> 00:00:20.367
"The ending is so tragic, Jo," Beth remarked, pulling her sweater tightly around herself.

4
00:00:20.368 --> 00:00:25.409
The sudden drop in temperatures had brought about a colder winter than anyone anticipated.

5
00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:31.407
"I felt this story should be more realistic; not so fanciful," the writer explained.

6
00:00:31.408 --> 00:00:38.731
"Well, I think you should have made it happier," criticized the artist, as she thumped a lump of clay on the table to soften it.

7
00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:46.323
Amy had moved on to experimenting with clay, and was molding small sculptures of everyone and everything these days.

8
00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:52.300
Mother’s familiar footsteps coming up the stairs outside sent the girls scurrying along to welcome her home.

9
00:00:52.301 --> 00:00:58.834
Tossing the newspaper onto the sofa, Beth raced to the door as Meg plumped up the cushions on Mother’s chair

10
00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:02.209
and Jo ran to warm her slippers by the fireplace.

11
00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:06.323
Amy wiped the clay muck off her hands and cleaned up her mess.

12
00:01:06.324 --> 00:01:10.923
"Hello, my girls!" Mother came into the room, holding out her arms.

13
00:01:10.924 --> 00:01:15.731
Her daughters embraced her, forming a circle so Mother would warm up quickly.

14
00:01:15.732 --> 00:01:21.819
"I’d better see to dinner," Meg said, leaving her sisters tending Mother while she went to help Hannah.

15
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.304
"Do we have any letters from Father?" asked Beth.

16
00:01:25.305 --> 00:01:33.119
Only Beth remembered that it was letter day and Father always sent a message, whether it was brief or detailed, to his family.

17
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.000
"I checked but there wasn’t anything today," Mother said in a gentle voice.

18
00:01:38.001 --> 00:01:44.456
The girls grew silent, not wanting to ask questions that might result in answers they dreaded to hear.

19
00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:52.538
Sensing their uneasiness, Mother said, "It’s not easy to write every week, especially when the soldiers are moving around so much.

20
00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:55.347
I’m sure your father is just fine."

21
00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:59.006
A sudden knock on the door interrupted their conversation.

22
00:01:59.007 --> 00:02:04.570
Puzzled, Mother got up and opened the door to find a young man in uniform shivering in the cold.

23
00:02:04.571 --> 00:02:08.866
He held a thin square envelope in his hand.

24
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:14.030
"Mrs. March? Telegram for you," he said in an emotionless voice.

25
00:02:14.031 --> 00:02:21.124
Mother took the envelope with shaky hands but held onto Jo for support as she made her way back to her chair.

26
00:02:21.125 --> 00:02:28.562
"Jo, could you, please? I can’t . . ." Mother, who was usually calm and cool, sounded frightened like a little girl.

27
00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:39.002
Impatiently ripping the envelope open, Jo read the message on the telegram, "Mrs. March, your husband is very ill. Must come at once."

28
00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:43.497
Jo stifled a cry as the telegram dropped from her hands, falling to the floor.

29
00:02:43.498 --> 00:02:51.010
Mother sat erect but silent tears fell onto her lap while she tried to comfort Beth and Amy, who were wailing loudly.

30
00:02:51.011 --> 00:02:55.977
"What’s the matter?" Meg ran into the room, Hannah close at her heels.

31
00:02:55.978 --> 00:03:01.687
When Jo told them about the telegram, Meg collapsed onto the sofa, holding her head in her hands.

32
00:03:01.688 --> 00:03:07.037
Hannah cried, "Poor Mr. March. What are we to do?"

33
00:03:07.038 --> 00:03:13.883
The grieving period for Father was short since time was running out and Mother had to leave immediately to be with him.

34
00:03:13.884 --> 00:03:17.753
"Meg, pack extra socks and sweaters for Father.

35
00:03:17.754 --> 00:03:21.747
Beth, go downstairs and ask Hannah for my medicine bag."

36
00:03:21.748 --> 00:03:26.545
At that moment, the doorbell rang and Amy went to see who it was.

37
00:03:26.546 --> 00:03:33.492
When Mother and Meg came downstairs, they recognized the voices of Mr. Laurence, Laurie, and Mr. Brookes.

38
00:03:33.493 --> 00:03:37.802
"Mrs. March, young Amy just told us the terrible news.

39
00:03:37.803 --> 00:03:43.264
We are at your service to do whatever is needed in order to help you and your girls,"

40
00:03:43.265 --> 00:03:48.874
Mr. Laurence offered kindly, taking Mother’s arm as he led her into the living room.

41
00:03:48.875 --> 00:03:54.384
"How did you know?" Meg asked Mr. Brookes, who also extended his arm to help her.

42
00:03:54.385 --> 00:04:03.207
"Laurie saw a soldier walking toward your house, and we all know that the army only sends a soldier if something is seriously wrong."

43
00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:09.079
Mother bowed her head in gratitude saying: "I am always grateful for your help, Mr. Laurence."

44
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:18.853
Shaking his head, he replied, "Please don’t mention it since it’s not only our duty but also our honor to help friends in their time of need."

45
00:04:18.854 --> 00:04:23.997
"Would you allow me to go with you so that you’re not traveling alone?" asked Mr. Brookes.

46
00:04:23.998 --> 00:04:28.516
Mother’s eyes filled with tears as she nodded, accepting his gracious offer.

47
00:04:28.517 --> 00:04:37.628
"Now, Mrs. March, time is crucial," said Mr. Laurence firmly, "so I think you should prepare your things and leave as soon as possible."

48
00:04:37.629 --> 00:04:43.848
"Grandfather, shall I ask Jenson to get the carriage ready?" asked Laurie, as he made his way to the door.

49
00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:53.902
"Yes, and make sure Cook has prepared enough food and drink for the journey," Mr. Laurence called out as his grandson left to speak with the stable manager.

50
00:04:53.903 --> 00:04:56.479
Within an hour Mother was ready to leave.

51
00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:01.068
Doing a last-minute check, she looked around to see if she had everything she needed.

52
00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:05.059
"Where’s Jo?" Mother asked, unable to see her second daughter.

53
00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.760
"She’s somewhere, I’m sure," said Meg, helping Mother put her coat on.

54
00:05:09.761 --> 00:05:15.979
"Tell Father we love him," said Beth, trying with difficulty to control her tears.

55
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.910
"We’re praying for his rapid recovery," said Amy.

56
00:05:19.911 --> 00:05:27.055
"Girls, you must mind Meg and Jo, and don’t forget the Hummels, who need our help as much as anyone," said Mother.

57
00:05:27.056 --> 00:05:30.541
"The carriage is ready," said Laurie, poking his head in the door.

58
00:05:30.542 --> 00:05:36.130
As Mother turned to say good-bye, Jo, shoving Laurie forcefully, suddenly burst in through the door.

59
00:05:36.131 --> 00:05:39.684
She was wearing a bonnet tied carelessly around her head.

60
00:05:39.685 --> 00:05:42.136
"I made it in time," she gasped.

61
00:05:42.137 --> 00:05:45.909
"Where have you been, Jo?" Meg asked in an annoyed voice.

62
00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:51.556
"To get something to contribute to Father’s well-being," Jo answered in a trembling voice.

63
00:05:51.557 --> 00:05:58.595
She handed Mother some bills and continued, "It’s not much, Mother, but I don’t have anything else to give."

64
00:05:58.596 --> 00:06:04.796
Counting the money, Mother questioned her daughter, "This is a lot, Jo. Where did you get it from?"

65
00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:10.358
Untying her bonnet’s ribbons, Jo let the bonnet slide off her head as she stood in silence.

66
00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:13.656
"Jo! Your hair!" screamed Amy.

67
00:06:13.657 --> 00:06:19.776
"I had no choice," explained Jo, running her fingers through the short, boyish cut, and continued,

68
00:06:19.777 --> 00:06:24.048
"I remember that the barber said he bought hair and used it to make wigs.

69
00:06:24.049 --> 00:06:29.829
So . . ." her voice broke, but she bravely carried on, "I sold it."

70
00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:37.545
Mother held Jo tightly in her arms and whispered, "Your father will know of your sacrifice and he will be so proud of you."

71
00:06:37.546 --> 00:06:40.435
"I’m sorry, but you must leave immediately,"

72
00:06:40.436 --> 00:06:47.430
Laurie interrupted gently, deliberately avoiding Jo’s eyes since he had not decided yet how he felt about her new haircut.

73
00:06:47.431 --> 00:06:52.270
Not wanting Mother to worry, the girls forced themselves to smile and look hopeful.

74
00:06:52.271 --> 00:06:59.167
As soon as Mother had closed the door behind her, however, the March sisters all broke down weeping.

